          ],
          "content": "Curiosity | Textbook of Science | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.019,
            0.926,
            0.052,
            0.944
          ],
          "content": "52"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.431,
            0.322,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 4.7(a): Deflagrating spoon"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.083,
            0.885,
            0.176
          ],
          "content": "Do you know that sodium is a metal which is stored in kerosene because it reacts vigorously with oxygen and water. A lot of heat is generated in the reaction. Storing sodium in kerosene prevents its exposure to moisture and air. Can you predict the nature of its oxide?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.178,
            0.883,
            0.216
          ],
          "content": "Let us now discuss some substances that do not behave like metals."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.235,
            0.86,
            0.287
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.4 Substances that Behave Differently from Metals in Air and Water"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.279,
            0.319,
            0.831,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": "**Activity 4.7: Let us experiment (demonstration activity)**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.334,
            0.364,
            0.755,
            0.384
          ],
          "content": "The teacher may demonstrate this activity."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.329,
            0.384,
            0.883,
            0.438
          ],
          "content": "**Caution**— This activity must be performed in a fume hood or well-ventilated area. Burning sulfur produces gases, which can be harmful if inhaled."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.474,
            0.301,
            0.604
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.134,
                0.605,
                0.318,
                0.64
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.7(b): Improvised deflagrating spoon"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.346,
            0.439,
            0.885,
            0.57
          ],
          "content": "Take a small amount of powdered sulfur in a deflagrating spoon (it is a long-handled metal spoon used in experiments to safely heat and burn substances Fig. 4.7a). If a deflagrating spoon is not available, you may take a metallic cap of any bottle, wrap a metallic wire around it and give it the shape as shown in Fig. 4.7b."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.347,
            0.573,
            0.884,
            0.668
          ],
          "content": "Heat it on a flame, and as soon as the sulfur starts burning, introduce the deflagrating spoon into a gas jar or glass tumbler (Fig. 4.7c). Cover the gas jar or glass tumbler with a lid to ensure that the gas produced does not escape."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.068,
            0.646,
            0.352,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.101,
                0.879,
                0.329,
                0.898
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.7(c): Burning of sulfur"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_11_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.347,
            0.67,
            0.884,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": "Remove the lid after 3 – 4 minutes and take out the deflagrating spoon. Add a small quantity of water into the gas jar, quickly place the lid back and shake it so that the gas dissolves."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.349,
            0.75,
            0.884,
            0.843
          ],
          "content": "Again, recall the chapter ‘Exploring Substances: Acidic, Basic, and Neutral’. Using an acid-base indicator, check whether the solution obtained after the addition of water to the gas jar is acidic or basic or neutral."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.348,
            0.848,
            0.6,
            0.869
          ],
          "content": "What do you observe?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.002,
            0.919,
            0.271,
